package esp_twai

import "std/embedded/hal"

// The Two-Wire Automotive Interface (TWAI) controller of Espressif's chips:
// TWAI0 and TWAI1 of the ESP32-C6, and the one controller of the ESP32-C3,
// S3, and H2. It is a CAN 2.0B controller modelled on the NXP SJA1000 in
// its PeliCAN mode: classic CAN frames with standard (11-bit) and extended
// (29-bit) identifiers at up to 1 Mbit/s, one transmit buffer, a 64-byte
// receive FIFO, and one acceptance filter. TWAI has no CAN FD. `Controller`
// implements hal.Can over any hal.Registers: a `hal.RegisterBlock` on the
// chip, or a simulated controller in a desktop test. Like any CAN
// controller it needs a transceiver (such as the SN65HVD230 or TJA1051) to
// reach a real bus; chips can also join several controllers on one wire
// without transceivers for short links and self-tests (see the chip
// package).
//
//     config := esp_twai.Config.new(500_000)
//     filters: [1]hal.CanFilter = [hal.CanFilter.exact(0x123, false)]
//     can := b.pins.can(tx, rx, &config, &filters)!
//     hal.send_frame(&mut can, &frame, hal.DEFAULT_POLL_LIMIT)!
//
// Bit timing. The controller counts time quanta of 2 * (BRP + 1) cycles of
// its function clock (the 40 MHz crystal on the ESP32-C6), BRP 0 to 16383,
// and a bit is a sync quantum, segment 1 of 1 to 16 quanta (TSEG1), and
// segment 2 of 1 to 8 (TSEG2), with a synchronization jump width of 1 to 4
// (BUS_TIMING_0 and BUS_TIMING_1). `timing` picks them with hal.can_timing
// within `limits` (segment 1 and 2 at least 2 quanta, as CAN requires for
// the information processing time): from 40 MHz, 1 Mbit/s, 800, 500, 250,
// 125, 100, 50, 20, and 10 kbit/s are exact, down to 50 bit/s. Config sets
// the sample point (875 thousandths unless set) and triple sampling.
//
// Modes. Normal mode takes part in the bus. SelfTest mode sends without
// needing an acknowledge and receives its own frames back through the
// filters (the self reception request), so one controller checks itself
// with nothing but its TX pad looped to its RX pad. ListenOnly mode receives
// without ever driving the bus: no acknowledges, no error frames, and
// poll_send reports Unsupported. `one_shot` sends each frame once, without
// the automatic retransmission after an error or lost arbitration.
//
// Filters. The hardware has one acceptance filter: 32 code bits and 32 mask
// bits (ACR0-3, AMR0-3, in the data registers while in reset mode), used
// either as one filter (single mode: a standard frame's identifier, RTR
// bit, and first two data bytes, or an extended frame's whole identifier
// and RTR bit) or as two (dual mode: two standard identifiers, or the top
// 16 bits of two extended identifiers). It does not tell standard from
// extended frames: the same bits are compared with either kind's
// identifier. hal.CanFilter cannot always be expressed exactly, so the
// controller programs the hardware filter to pass at least every frame the
// filters pass, and then checks each received frame against the filters in
// software, so poll_receive returns exactly the frames hal.CanFilter.accepts
// for some filter. The hardware part only spares the receive FIFO:
//
//   no filters       every frame passes
//   standard only    dual mode: the first filter exactly, the others merged
//                    into the second hardware filter (only the identifier
//                    bits all of them agree on are compared)
//   one extended     single mode, exact for extended frames
//   extended only    the merged filter in single mode, or the first filter
//                    and the merged rest on their top 16 bits in dual mode,
//                    whichever compares more bits
//   both kinds       dual mode: the merged standard filters in the first,
//                    the top 12 bits of the merged extended filters in the
//                    second
//
// Frames the software check drops still took room in the FIFO, and every
// controller acknowledges every valid frame on the bus whatever its filters.
// Up to MAX_FILTERS filters; InvalidInput for more, or for an identifier
// too large for its kind.
//
// Receiving. The FIFO holds 64 bytes: frames take 3 bytes plus their data
// (standard) or 5 plus their data (extended), so it holds 5 standard frames
// of 8 bytes or 4 extended ones. When a frame arrives with the FIFO full it
// is lost and the controller marks its place (MISS_ST): poll_receive
// returns the frames before it, then reports Overrun once, and then the
// frames received after room was made. poll_receive reads at most one
// frame the filters pass per call, skipping the ones they drop.
//
// Sending. There is one transmit buffer: poll_send writes a frame into it
// and requests transmission, and reports false while the previous frame is
// still pending. In Normal mode a frame is retried until another node
// acknowledges it, so on a bus with no other node the buffer stays full;
// `abort_pending` cancels it.
//
// Errors. The transmit and receive error counters (TEC, REC) decide
// error_state: Passive from 128, and BusOff once TEC passes 255. A bus-off
// controller enters reset mode and stops; the controller then starts
// recovery at once (the default, Config.automatic_recovery), or when
// `recover` is called. Recovery leaves reset mode, after which the
// controller waits for 128 runs of 11 recessive bits and becomes error
// active with both counters 0. poll_send reports Bus while bus-off.
//
// Register names follow the ESP32-C6 technical reference manual (Two-wire
// Automotive Interface).

// Registers, 4 bytes apart (the SJA1000's byte registers, one per word).
const MODE: usize = 0x00
const CMD: usize = 0x04
const STATUS: usize = 0x08
const INT_RAW: usize = 0x0C
const INT_ENA: usize = 0x10
const BUS_TIMING_0: usize = 0x18
const BUS_TIMING_1: usize = 0x1C
const ERR_CODE_CAP: usize = 0x30
const ERR_WARNING_LIMIT: usize = 0x34
const RX_ERR_CNT: usize = 0x38
const TX_ERR_CNT: usize = 0x3C
// DATA_0 to DATA_12: the frame in operating mode (the receive buffer when
// read, the transmit buffer when written), the acceptance code (ACR0-3)
// and mask (AMR0-3) in DATA_0 to DATA_7 in reset mode.
const DATA: usize = 0x40
const RX_MESSAGE_CNT: usize = 0x74
const CLOCK_DIVIDER: usize = 0x7C

// MODE: reset mode, listen-only mode, self-test mode, and single filter
// mode.
const RESET_MODE: u32 = 1 << 0
const LISTEN_ONLY_MODE: u32 = 1 << 1
const SELF_TEST_MODE: u32 = 1 << 2
const SINGLE_FILTER: u32 = 1 << 3
// CMD: transmission request, abort transmission, release the receive
// buffer, clear the data overrun flag, and self reception request.
const TX_REQ: u32 = 1 << 0
const ABORT_TX: u32 = 1 << 1
const RELEASE_BUF: u32 = 1 << 2
const CLR_OVERRUN: u32 = 1 << 3
const SELF_RX_REQ: u32 = 1 << 4
// STATUS: a frame waits in the FIFO, data overrun, the transmit buffer is
// free, the last transmission completed, receiving, transmitting, an error
// counter reached the warning limit, bus-off, and the frame at the head of
// the FIFO was lost to an overrun.
const RX_BUF_ST: u32 = 1 << 0
const OVERRUN_ST: u32 = 1 << 1
const TX_BUF_ST: u32 = 1 << 2
const TX_COMPLETE_ST: u32 = 1 << 3
const ERR_ST: u32 = 1 << 6
const BUS_OFF_ST: u32 = 1 << 7
const MISS_ST: u32 = 1 << 8
// CLOCK_DIVIDER: the CLKOUT pin off.
const CLOCK_OFF: u32 = 1 << 8
// BUS_TIMING_1: sample each bit three times.
const TRIPLE_SAMPLING: u32 = 1 << 7

// The frame information byte: an extended frame (FF), a remote frame
// (RTR), and the data length code.
const FRAME_FORMAT: u8 = 0x80
const FRAME_REMOTE: u8 = 0x40
const FRAME_DLC: u8 = 0x0F

// Interrupt sources for listen and take_interrupts (INT_ENA and INT_RAW
// bits): a frame waits (RIE), the transmit buffer became free (TIE), the
// error warning or bus-off status changed (EIE), a data overrun (DOIE), the
// controller became error passive or active again (EPIE), it lost
// arbitration (ALIE), and it saw a bus error (BEIE).
pub const RECEIVE: u32 = 1 << 0
pub const TRANSMIT: u32 = 1 << 1
pub const ERROR_WARNING: u32 = 1 << 2
pub const OVERRUN: u32 = 1 << 3
pub const ERROR_PASSIVE: u32 = 1 << 5
pub const ARBITRATION_LOST: u32 = 1 << 6
pub const BUS_ERROR: u32 = 1 << 7

// The most filters a controller takes.
pub const MAX_FILTERS: usize = 8
// Status reads before abort_pending gives up with Timeout. Aborting takes
// effect at once unless a frame is on the bus, which ends within 160 bit
// times.
pub const ABORT_POLLS: u32 = 100_000
// The error warning limit (ERR_WARNING_LIMIT), the counter level that sets
// STATUS.ERR_ST: the SJA1000's default.
pub const WARNING_LIMIT: u32 = 96

// Operating modes after configuration.
pub enum Mode {
    // Take part in the bus: send, receive, and acknowledge frames.
    Normal,
    // Send without needing an acknowledge and receive one's own frames,
    // through the filters; for checking a controller with its TX looped to
    // its RX and no other node.
    SelfTest,
    // Receive every valid frame but never send, acknowledge, or signal
    // errors; for monitoring a bus or detecting its bit rate.
    ListenOnly
}

// How `Controller.new` and `configure` set the controller up.
pub struct Config {
    pub bitrate: u32
    // Where to sample each bit, in thousandths of the bit; see "Bit
    // timing".
    pub sample_point_permille: u32
    pub mode: Mode
    // Send each frame once, without retrying after an error or a lost
    // arbitration. The transmit buffer is free afterwards either way.
    pub one_shot: bool
    // Sample each bit three times and take the majority, for slow, noisy
    // buses; CiA recommends a single sample above 125 kbit/s.
    pub triple_sampling: bool
    // Start recovering from bus-off as soon as the controller is seen
    // bus-off, instead of when `recover` is called.
    pub automatic_recovery: bool

    // Normal mode at `bitrate`, sampling once at 87.5%, retrying frames,
    // recovering from bus-off automatically.
    pub fn new(bitrate: u32) -> Config {
        return Config { bitrate, sample_point_permille: 875, mode: Mode.Normal, one_shot: false, triple_sampling: false, automatic_recovery: true }
    }
}

// The TWAI bit timing registers for one bit rate.
pub struct Timing {
    // BUS_TIMING_0: the synchronization jump width less one (bits 15-14)
    // and the baud rate prescaler BRP (bits 13-0).
    pub bus_timing_0: u32
    // BUS_TIMING_1: segment 2 less one (bits 6-4) and segment 1 less one
    // (bits 3-0); `configure` adds triple sampling (bit 7).
    pub bus_timing_1: u32
    // The bit rate the registers give, rounded, and the sample point.
    pub bitrate: u32
    pub sample_point_permille: u32
}

// The ranges hal.can_timing may choose from for TWAI: prescalers of 2 to
// 32768 clock cycles in steps of 2, segment 1 of 2 to 16 quanta, segment 2
// of 2 to 8, and a jump width up to 4.
pub fn limits() -> hal.CanTimingLimits {
    return hal.CanTimingLimits { prescaler_min: 2, prescaler_max: 32768, prescaler_step: 2, segment1_min: 2, segment1_max: 16, segment2_min: 2, segment2_max: 8, sync_jump_max: 4 }
}

// The timing registers for `bitrate` from a function clock of `clock_hz`,
// sampling near `sample_point_permille`; see "Bit timing" above.
// InvalidInput if no timing reaches the bit rate within 0.5%.
pub fn timing(clock_hz: u32, bitrate: u32, sample_point_permille: u32) -> Timing!hal.Error {
    range := limits()
    bit := hal.can_timing(clock_hz, bitrate, sample_point_permille, &range)?
    brp := bit.prescaler / 2 - 1
    zero := (((bit.sync_jump - 1) & 0x3) << 14) | (brp & 0x3FFF)
    one := (((bit.segment2 - 1) & 0x7) << 4) | ((bit.segment1 - 1) & 0xF)
    return ok(Timing { bus_timing_0: zero, bus_timing_1: one, bitrate: bit.bitrate(clock_hz), sample_point_permille: bit.sample_point_permille() })
}

// The hardware acceptance filter: single or dual mode, and the acceptance
// code and mask as one 32-bit value each, ACR0 and AMR0 in the top byte.
// Mask bits set are bits the filter ignores (the hardware's sense).
pub struct FilterPlan {
    pub single: bool
    pub code: u32
    pub mask: u32
}

// The hardware filter for `filters`; see "Filters" above. InvalidInput for
// more than MAX_FILTERS filters or an identifier too large for its kind.
pub fn plan_filters(filters: &[hal.CanFilter]) -> FilterPlan!hal.Error {
    if filters.len > MAX_FILTERS {
        return err(hal.Error.InvalidInput)
    }
    standard := 0usize
    extended := 0usize
    for index in 0..filters.len {
        filter := &filters[index]
        if filter.extended {
            if filter.id > hal.CAN_MAX_EXTENDED_ID {
                return err(hal.Error.InvalidInput)
            }
            extended += 1
        } else {
            if filter.id > hal.CAN_MAX_STANDARD_ID {
                return err(hal.Error.InvalidInput)
            }
            standard += 1
        }
    }
    if filters.len == 0 {
        return ok(FilterPlan { single: true, code: 0, mask: 0xFFFF_FFFF })
    }
    if extended == 0 {
        // Dual mode: the first standard filter, then the rest merged. The
        // RTR bit and the data nibbles stay unchecked.
        first := Merge.new()
        rest := Merge.new()
        for index in 0..filters.len {
            if first.count == 0 {
                first.add(filters[index].id, filters[index].mask & hal.CAN_MAX_STANDARD_ID)
            } else {
                rest.add(filters[index].id, filters[index].mask & hal.CAN_MAX_STANDARD_ID)
            }
        }
        if rest.count == 0 {
            rest.add(first.code, first.care)
        }
        code := (first.code << 21) | (rest.code << 5)
        care := (first.care << 21) | (rest.care << 5)
        return ok(FilterPlan { single: false, code, mask: ~care })
    }
    if standard == 0 {
        all := Merge.new()
        first := Merge.new()
        rest := Merge.new()
        for index in 0..filters.len {
            mask := filters[index].mask & hal.CAN_MAX_EXTENDED_ID
            all.add(filters[index].id, mask)
            if first.count == 0 {
                first.add(filters[index].id, mask)
            } else {
                rest.add(filters[index].id, mask)
            }
        }
        if rest.count == 0 || count_bits(all.care) >= smaller(count_bits(first.care >> 13), count_bits(rest.care >> 13)) {
            // Single mode: the identifier in bits 31-3, RTR unchecked.
            return ok(FilterPlan { single: true, code: all.code << 3, mask: ~(all.care << 3) })
        }
        code := ((first.code >> 13) << 16) | ((rest.code >> 13) & 0xFFFF)
        care := ((first.care >> 13) << 16) | ((rest.care >> 13) & 0xFFFF)
        return ok(FilterPlan { single: false, code, mask: ~care })
    }
    // Both kinds: dual mode, the standard filters in the first hardware
    // filter and the top 16 bits of the extended ones in the second.
    short := Merge.new()
    long := Merge.new()
    for index in 0..filters.len {
        if filters[index].extended {
            long.add(filters[index].id, filters[index].mask & hal.CAN_MAX_EXTENDED_ID)
        } else {
            short.add(filters[index].id, filters[index].mask & hal.CAN_MAX_STANDARD_ID)
        }
    }
    // ACR3's low nibble is also the first filter's check of a standard
    // frame's first data byte, so the extended filter keeps its top 12
    // bits.
    code := (short.code << 21) | ((long.code >> 13) & 0xFFF0)
    care := (short.care << 21) | ((long.care >> 13) & 0xFFF0)
    return ok(FilterPlan { single: false, code, mask: ~care })
}

// Filters merged into one code and mask: only the bits every filter checks
// and agrees on stay checked ("care" bits).
struct Merge {
    code: u32
    care: u32
    count: u32

    fn new() -> Merge {
        return Merge { code: 0, care: 0, count: 0 }
    }

    fn add(&mut self, id: u32, mask: u32) {
        if self.count == 0 {
            self.care = mask
        } else {
            self.care = self.care & mask & ~(id ^ self.code)
        }
        self.code = id & self.care
        self.count += 1
    }
}

fn count_bits(value: u32) -> u32 {
    count := 0u32
    rest := value
    for rest != 0 {
        rest = rest & (rest - 1)
        count += 1
    }
    return count
}

fn smaller(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {
    if a < b {
        return a
    }
    return b
}

// A frame's 13 bytes as the data registers hold it: the frame information,
// the identifier (2 bytes standard, 4 extended, left-aligned), and the
// data. Returns how many bytes the frame uses.
pub fn encode_frame(frame: &hal.CanFrame, bytes: &mut [13]u8) -> usize {
    info := frame.dlc() & FRAME_DLC
    if frame.is_remote() {
        info = info | FRAME_REMOTE
    }
    id := frame.id()
    start := 3usize
    if frame.is_extended() {
        info = info | FRAME_FORMAT
        raw := id << 3
        bytes[1] = (raw >> 24) as u8
        bytes[2] = ((raw >> 16) & 0xFF) as u8
        bytes[3] = ((raw >> 8) & 0xFF) as u8
        bytes[4] = (raw & 0xFF) as u8
        start = 5
    } else {
        raw := id << 5
        bytes[1] = ((raw >> 8) & 0xFF) as u8
        bytes[2] = (raw & 0xFF) as u8
    }
    bytes[0] = info
    data := frame.bytes()
    for index in 0..data.len {
        bytes[start + index] = data[index]
    }
    return start + data.len
}

// Make `frame` the frame in `bytes`, as the data registers hold it. A data
// length code above 8 means 8 bytes, as classic CAN defines it.
pub fn decode_frame(bytes: &[13]u8, frame: &mut hal.CanFrame) -> void!hal.Error {
    info := bytes[0]
    length := hal.can_dlc_length(info & FRAME_DLC, false)
    extended := (info & FRAME_FORMAT) != 0
    id := 0u32
    start := 3usize
    if extended {
        raw := ((bytes[1] as u32) << 24) | ((bytes[2] as u32) << 16) | ((bytes[3] as u32) << 8) | bytes[4] as u32
        id = raw >> 3
        start = 5
    } else {
        id = (((bytes[1] as u32) << 8) | bytes[2] as u32) >> 5
    }
    if (info & FRAME_REMOTE) != 0 {
        return frame.set_remote(id, extended, length)
    }
    return frame.set_data(id, extended, bytes[start..start + length])
}

// A TWAI controller over registers R, from `new` until `release`.
pub struct Controller<R: hal.Registers>: hal.Can {
    registers: R
    // The MODE bits of the operating mode: listen-only or self-test, and
    // single filter mode.
    mode_bits: u32
    one_shot: bool
    automatic_recovery: bool
    // The filters, for the software check: identifier and mask, and a bit
    // per filter for extended ones.
    filter_ids: [8]u32
    filter_masks: [8]u32
    filter_extended: u32
    filter_count: usize

    // Take registers R of a controller that is already running, keeping
    // its configuration, with no filters for the software check and
    // automatic bus-off recovery; `configure` sets it up. Most programs
    // call `new` instead.
    pub fn attach(registers: R) -> Controller<R> {
        can := Controller<R> { registers, mode_bits: 0, one_shot: false, automatic_recovery: true, filter_ids: [0u32; 8], filter_masks: [0u32; 8], filter_extended: 0, filter_count: 0 }
        can.mode_bits = can.registers.read(MODE) & (LISTEN_ONLY_MODE | SELF_TEST_MODE | SINGLE_FILTER)
        return can
    }

    // Configure the controller with registers R, clocked at `clock_hz`
    // (the 40 MHz crystal on the ESP32-C6), from `config` and `filters`;
    // see `configure`. InvalidInput as for `configure`, before the
    // controller is touched.
    pub fn new(registers: R, clock_hz: u32, config: &Config, filters: &[hal.CanFilter]) -> Controller<R>!hal.Error {
        can := Controller<R> { registers, mode_bits: 0, one_shot: false, automatic_recovery: true, filter_ids: [0u32; 8], filter_masks: [0u32; 8], filter_extended: 0, filter_count: 0 }
        can.configure(clock_hz, config, filters)?
        return ok(can)
    }

    // Set the controller up from `config` and `filters`: enter reset mode,
    // which drops a pending frame and the received ones, clear the error
    // counters, program the bit timing and the acceptance filter, and start
    // in the configured mode. InvalidInput for a bit rate the controller
    // cannot reach or filters it does not take (see "Filters"), before the
    // controller is touched.
    pub fn configure(&mut self, clock_hz: u32, config: &Config, filters: &[hal.CanFilter]) -> void!hal.Error {
        bits := timing(clock_hz, config.bitrate, config.sample_point_permille)?
        plan := plan_filters(filters)?
        self.filter_count = filters.len
        self.filter_extended = 0
        for index in 0..filters.len {
            self.filter_ids[index] = filters[index].id
            self.filter_masks[index] = filters[index].mask
            if filters[index].extended {
                self.filter_extended |= 1u32 << index as u32
            }
        }
        self.one_shot = config.one_shot
        self.automatic_recovery = config.automatic_recovery
        self.mode_bits = mode_bits(&config.mode)
        if plan.single {
            self.mode_bits |= SINGLE_FILTER
        }
        // Listen-only freezes the receive error counter while it is set.
        self.registers.write(MODE, RESET_MODE)
        self.registers.write(MODE, RESET_MODE | LISTEN_ONLY_MODE)
        self.registers.write(CLOCK_DIVIDER, CLOCK_OFF)
        self.registers.write(INT_ENA, 0)
        self.registers.write(TX_ERR_CNT, 0)
        self.registers.write(RX_ERR_CNT, 0)
        self.registers.write(ERR_WARNING_LIMIT, WARNING_LIMIT)
        self.registers.write(BUS_TIMING_0, bits.bus_timing_0)
        sampling := 0u32
        if config.triple_sampling {
            sampling = TRIPLE_SAMPLING
        }
        self.registers.write(BUS_TIMING_1, bits.bus_timing_1 | sampling)
        for index in 0usize..4 {
            shift := (24 - 8 * index) as u32
            self.registers.write(DATA + 4 * index, (plan.code >> shift) & 0xFF)
            self.registers.write(DATA + 16 + 4 * index, (plan.mask >> shift) & 0xFF)
        }
        self.registers.write(MODE, RESET_MODE | self.mode_bits)
        self.registers.write(MODE, self.mode_bits)
        return ok()
    }

    // Switch to `mode`, keeping the bit timing and filters. The controller
    // passes through reset mode, which drops a pending frame and the
    // frames in the FIFO; the error counters stay.
    pub fn set_mode(&mut self, mode: Mode) {
        filter := self.mode_bits & SINGLE_FILTER
        self.mode_bits = mode_bits(&mode) | filter
        current := self.registers.read(MODE)
        self.registers.write(MODE, current | RESET_MODE)
        self.registers.write(MODE, RESET_MODE | self.mode_bits)
        self.registers.write(MODE, self.mode_bits)
    }

    // Queue `frame` in the transmit buffer and request it; see hal.Can and
    // "Sending" above. False while the previous frame is pending.
    // Unsupported for an FD frame, as TWAI sends classic CAN only, and in
    // ListenOnly mode; Bus while the controller is bus-off.
    pub fn poll_send(&mut self, frame: &hal.CanFrame) -> bool!hal.Error {
        if frame.is_fd() || (self.mode_bits & LISTEN_ONLY_MODE) != 0 {
            return err(hal.Error.Unsupported)
        }
        status := self.registers.read(STATUS)
        if (status & BUS_OFF_ST) != 0 || self.in_reset() {
            self.recover_if_automatic()
            return err(hal.Error.Bus)
        }
        if (status & TX_BUF_ST) == 0 {
            return ok(false)
        }
        bytes := [0u8; 13]
        used := encode_frame(frame, &mut bytes)
        for index in 0..used {
            self.registers.write(DATA + 4 * index, bytes[index] as u32)
        }
        command := TX_REQ
        if (self.mode_bits & SELF_TEST_MODE) != 0 {
            command = SELF_RX_REQ
        }
        if self.one_shot {
            command |= ABORT_TX
        }
        self.registers.write(CMD, command)
        return ok(true)
    }

    // Take the oldest received frame the filters pass into `frame`; see
    // hal.Can and "Receiving" above. False while none waits, and while the
    // controller is bus-off. Overrun once where frames were lost.
    pub fn poll_receive(&mut self, frame: &mut hal.CanFrame) -> bool!hal.Error {
        // Each pass takes one frame from the FIFO, which holds at most 21.
        for _ in 0u32..32 {
            if self.in_reset() {
                self.recover_if_automatic()
                return ok(false)
            }
            status := self.registers.read(STATUS)
            if (status & RX_BUF_ST) == 0 {
                if (status & OVERRUN_ST) != 0 {
                    self.registers.write(CMD, CLR_OVERRUN)
                    return err(hal.Error.Overrun)
                }
                return ok(false)
            }
            if (status & MISS_ST) != 0 {
                // Drop every lost frame's place, then report them once.
                for _ in 0u32..32 {
                    self.registers.write(CMD, RELEASE_BUF)
                    next := self.registers.read(STATUS)
                    if (next & RX_BUF_ST) == 0 || (next & MISS_ST) == 0 {
                        break
                    }
                }
                self.registers.write(CMD, CLR_OVERRUN)
                return err(hal.Error.Overrun)
            }
            bytes := [0u8; 13]
            bytes[0] = (self.registers.read(DATA) & 0xFF) as u8
            used := 3usize
            if (bytes[0] & FRAME_FORMAT) != 0 {
                used = 5
            }
            if (bytes[0] & FRAME_REMOTE) == 0 {
                used += hal.can_dlc_length(bytes[0] & FRAME_DLC, false)
            }
            for index in 1..used {
                bytes[index] = (self.registers.read(DATA + 4 * index) & 0xFF) as u8
            }
            self.registers.write(CMD, RELEASE_BUF)
            decode_frame(&bytes, frame)?
            if self.passes(frame) {
                return ok(true)
            }
        }
        return ok(false)
    }

    // The fault confinement state: BusOff while STATUS.BUS_OFF_ST is set
    // (starting recovery if automatic), Passive while either error counter
    // is 128 or more, else Active.
    pub fn error_state(&mut self) -> hal.CanErrorState!hal.Error {
        if (self.registers.read(STATUS) & BUS_OFF_ST) != 0 {
            self.recover_if_automatic()
            return ok(hal.CanErrorState.BusOff)
        }
        if self.tec() >= 128 || self.rec() >= 128 {
            return ok(hal.CanErrorState.Passive)
        }
        return ok(hal.CanErrorState.Active)
    }

    // The transmit error counter (TEC): error-passive from 128, bus-off
    // after 255. During bus-off recovery it counts the runs of 11 recessive
    // bits still to come down from 127.
    pub fn tec(&mut self) -> u32 {
        return self.registers.read(TX_ERR_CNT) & 0xFF
    }

    // The receive error counter (REC): error-passive from 128.
    pub fn rec(&mut self) -> u32 {
        return self.registers.read(RX_ERR_CNT) & 0xFF
    }

    // The status register (STATUS), for the bits error_state does not
    // report, such as ERR_ST (bit 6: a counter reached WARNING_LIMIT).
    pub fn status(&mut self) -> u32 {
        return self.registers.read(STATUS)
    }

    // The frames waiting in the FIFO, including the places of lost ones
    // (RX_MESSAGE_CNT).
    pub fn waiting(&mut self) -> u32 {
        return self.registers.read(RX_MESSAGE_CNT) & 0x7F
    }

    // Whether a frame waits in the transmit buffer.
    pub fn is_pending(&mut self) -> bool {
        return (self.registers.read(STATUS) & TX_BUF_ST) == 0
    }

    // The last bus error the controller saw (ERR_CODE_CAP): its segment
    // (bits 4-0), whether it was receiving (bit 5), and its kind (bits 7-6:
    // bit, form, stuff, or other error). Reading it lets the controller
    // capture the next error.
    pub fn error_code(&mut self) -> u32 {
        return self.registers.read(ERR_CODE_CAP) & 0xFF
    }

    // Cancel the frame waiting to be sent (ABORT_TX) and wait until the
    // transmit buffer is free; a frame already on the bus finishes first.
    // Timeout after ABORT_POLLS status reads.
    pub fn abort_pending(&mut self) -> void!hal.Error {
        self.registers.write(CMD, ABORT_TX)
        for _ in 0u32..ABORT_POLLS {
            if (self.registers.read(STATUS) & TX_BUF_ST) != 0 {
                return ok()
            }
        }
        return err(hal.Error.Timeout)
    }

    // Start recovering from bus-off: leave the reset mode the controller
    // entered, so it counts 128 runs of 11 recessive bits and becomes error
    // active. Does nothing unless the controller is bus-off.
    pub fn recover(&mut self) {
        mode := self.registers.read(MODE)
        if (self.registers.read(STATUS) & BUS_OFF_ST) != 0 && (mode & RESET_MODE) != 0 {
            self.registers.write(MODE, mode & ~RESET_MODE)
        }
    }

    // Raise the controller's interrupt while any of `sources` (RECEIVE,
    // TRANSMIT, ..., ORed together) is pending; replaces the set listened
    // to.
    pub fn listen(&mut self, sources: u32) {
        self.registers.write(INT_ENA, sources & 0xFF)
    }

    // The interrupt sources pending among those listened to, which reading
    // clears, except RECEIVE, which stays while a frame waits.
    pub fn take_interrupts(&mut self) -> u32 {
        return self.registers.read(INT_RAW) & 0x1FF
    }

    // Enter reset mode, which stops the controller, and return its
    // registers.
    pub fn release(self) -> R {
        self.registers.write(INT_ENA, 0)
        self.registers.write(MODE, RESET_MODE)
        let Controller { registers, mode_bits, one_shot, automatic_recovery, filter_ids, filter_masks, filter_extended, filter_count } = self
        return registers
    }

    fn in_reset(&mut self) -> bool {
        return (self.registers.read(MODE) & RESET_MODE) != 0
    }

    fn recover_if_automatic(&mut self) {
        if self.automatic_recovery {
            self.recover()
        }
    }

    // Whether some filter passes `frame`, or there are none.
    fn passes(&self, frame: &hal.CanFrame) -> bool {
        if self.filter_count == 0 {
            return true
        }
        for index in 0..self.filter_count {
            extended := (self.filter_extended & (1u32 << index as u32)) != 0
            if frame.is_extended() == extended && (frame.id() & self.filter_masks[index]) == (self.filter_ids[index] & self.filter_masks[index]) {
                return true
            }
        }
        return false
    }
}

fn mode_bits(mode: &Mode) -> u32 {
    match mode {
        Mode.Normal => {
            return 0
        }
        Mode.SelfTest => {
            return SELF_TEST_MODE
        }
        Mode.ListenOnly => {
            return LISTEN_ONLY_MODE
        }
    }
}
